\[
e_p(n!) = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^k} \right\rfloor
\]

We will calculate the exponents of the prime factors of \( 10! \) and then determine the maximum \( x \) such that \( x^3 \) divides \( 10! \). This involves ensuring that for each prime factor \( p \), the exponent \( e_p(10!) \) is at least \( 3k \) for some integer \( k \).

