The problem asks for the sum of the smallest five positive integers that each have exactly four positive factors. A number has exactly four factors if it is of the form \( p^3 \) or \( p_1 \times p_2 \). 

In the code, we first generate a list of prime numbers using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. We then compute numbers of the form \( p^3 \) and \( p_1 \times p_2 \) and store them in a set to avoid duplicates. Finally, we sort these numbers and sum the smallest five.

The result from Stage 3 is 66, confirming that the sum of the smallest five integers with exactly four factors is indeed correct.

