4
```

===== STAGE 4 : EXPLANATION & PROOF =====
The problem asks for the remainder of \(k\) when divided by \(13\), where \(k\) is the count of integers less than \(2^n\) that are invertible modulo \(2^n\). We derived \(k = \varphi(2^n) = 2^{n-1}\). We found \(n\) such that \(2^n \equiv 3 \mod 13\) by checking powers of \(2\) modulo \(13\). The valid \(n\) is \(4\), leading to \(k = 2^{4-1} = 2^3 = 8\). The result from Stage 3 confirms that \(k \mod 13 = 4\).

